Lvl 16 from kills translates to 1 280 000 score, double that when
ascending; total score from such an ascension would probably be under
3Mio. An ascension between 5,5Mio and 6Mio score translates to enough
experience to achieve level 17 (640K Exp). That's quite possible
without sitting around casting create monster for days.
An extinctionist can expect to collect well over 5Mio experience
points, that's enough for Xp:20. The highest scores ever were afaik
achieved by coralling in the Riders and killing them thousands of
times.

 What
> > > does everybody think about the idea of limiting
> > > scoring for monster kills?

I'd much prefer it to limit it by giving less score per kill, say a
mere Monsterlevel +1 (so you still get a point for slaying a newt).
Even if that gets doubled for ascension, killing a maxed-out Rodney
would give you no more than 100 points of final score instead of
something like 10 000.
I find that monster kills play too large a role in score calculation
as is; with massively reduced score per kill the problem would pretty
much disappear. The royal way to a huge score would still be
extinctionism, but only by dint of killing huge numbers of giants and
polypiling rocks and gems like mad.

I'd also like to get special score for "achievements": reaching
special levels - Oracle, Mine End, Quest, Castle, Valley, Sanctum;
each level of Sokoban -, doing special things like finishing the
quest, doing the Invocation, having sex ("scoring", y'know) etc.

I'd like it best if final score were made up of 50% treasure collected
and 50% achievements. And of the achievements, killing things ought to
be no more than 1/2. My suggestion of mlvl+1 would mean quite a bit
less than that, so perhaps a _bit_ more score per kill could be in
order.

Keeping track of special achievements would break savefile
compatibility, though; but in general, i find the current scoring
system unsatisfactory - too few parameters.

